(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-acid-base-disorders-ii-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Acid-Base Disorders II Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old woman takes hydrocortisone for a chronic inflammatory condition and must stay on a corticosteroid. She has a metabolic alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which change to her corticosteroid is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep hydrocortisone at the same dose","Switch to cortisone","Raise the hydrocortisone dose","Switch to prednisone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If a patient with metabolic alkalosis requires corticosteroids, lower the dose or change to one with less mineralocorticoid activity: prednisone or methylprednisolone rather than cortisone or hydrocortisone. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had obesity hypoventilation syndrome for many years. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 66 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). For respiratory acidosis, bicarbonate is expected to rise 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide when acute and 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg when chronic, from baselines of 40 mm Hg and 25 mEq/L. Which process accounts for his bicarbonate of 34?","choices":["The kidneys retaining bicarbonate over days","The lungs retaining carbon dioxide to compensate","A second, primary metabolic alkalosis","The kidneys excreting acid within minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A primary respiratory acidosis is compensated by the kidneys, which retain bicarbonate over hours to days. A chronic rise of 26 mm Hg predicts 25 + 3.5 x 2.6 = about 34, exactly his value, so this is the expected renal compensation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 19, 23, 24, 34"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with cirrhosis and a low albumin has had watery diarrhea for 6 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His albumin is 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). His anion gap calculates to 5. Which step comes next in interpreting the gap?","choices":["Screen for a plasma cell dyscrasia","Check for bromide intoxication","Search the raised-gap causes","Correct the gap for his low albumin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low gap is most often a low albumin, and the gap must be corrected when albumin is abnormal. Adding 2.5 for each 1 g/dL below 4.5 gives 5 + 5 = 10, a normal gap, which fits a normal-gap metabolic acidosis from his diarrhea. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 46, 48, 57"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with uremia from kidney failure has vomited for 5 days and is dry on examination. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.41 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 41 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 25 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 143 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 97 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Replace fluids and treat the kidney failure","Give bicarbonate to treat the acidosis","No treatment since his pH is normal","Treat the vomiting alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 143 - (97 + 25) = 21, change in gap = 11, change in bicarbonate = 0: a uremic acidosis with a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. Both causes need treatment: replace his fluid deficit for the vomiting and treat the uremia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 63, 30, 54, 23"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with nephrotic syndrome and a urinary infection has a serum albumin 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 37 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 20 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, what is her change in anion gap once the gap is corrected for albumin?","choices":["4","9","14","19"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (104 + 20) = 14. Albumin is 2 below 4.5, so add 2.5 x 2 = 5: corrected gap = 19. Change in gap = 19 - 10 = 9. Against a change in bicarbonate of 25 - 20 = 5, the gap rose more. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 8, 57"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman in the intensive care unit with septic shock is febrile, hypotensive and breathing 32 times a minute. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (104 + 12) = 22, raised, so a metabolic acidosis is present despite the normal pH. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28; her 20 is below it, so a concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present. Sepsis is the classic setting, and the two pull the pH back to normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 26, 28, 49"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with pneumonia and septic shock is hypoxic and breathing 34 times a minute. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.38 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 21 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). What does his carbon dioxide indicate?","choices":["A concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate respiratory compensation","A concurrent respiratory acidosis","A concurrent metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 139 - (104 + 12) = 23, raised. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28. His 21 is below it, so hypoxia and sepsis are driving extra ventilation, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis that brings the pH back to 7.38. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 28, 39, 50, 23"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with weeks of vomiting from a gastric outlet obstruction has muscle weakness and hyporeflexia (diminished reflexes). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia","Respiratory alkalosis with low potassium","Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia","Respiratory acidosis from muscle weakness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.50 with a bicarbonate of 36 is a metabolic alkalosis; vomiting causes it by loss of gastric acid. The carbon dioxide of 48 is compensation. Hypokalemia, partly a shift into cells, causes the weakness and hyporeflexia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 51, 52, 53, 58"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with generalized anxiety disorder and interstitial pulmonary fibrosis is brought in after a car crash with a flail chest (a rib segment moving inward on inspiration). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 56 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 27 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history explains this blood gas?","choices":["The flail chest","His anxiety disorder","His interstitial fibrosis","Pain from his injuries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a high carbon dioxide is a respiratory acidosis, and the bicarbonate of 27 fits an acute rise. Respiratory acidosis comes from decreased ventilation, and thoracic cage injury such as a flail chest is a listed cause. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 31, 32, 39, 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with months of poor oral intake is admitted with a new infection. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 35 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 106 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her albumin is 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). What is her anion gap after correction for her albumin?","choices":["12","14.5","17","19.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The measured gap is 136 - (18 + 106) = 12. The correction adds 2.5 for each 1 g/dL that albumin sits below 4.5: 4.5 - 2.5 = 2, and 2 x 2.5 = 5, so the corrected gap is 12 + 5 = 17, above the normal 6 to 12. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 57"},{"q":"A 72-year-old malnourished woman from a nursing home has a urinary infection, hypotension and a respiratory rate of 30. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.44 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 26 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 134 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), albumin 1.7 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 134 - (105 + 17) = 12, which looks normal, but corrected gap = 12 + 2.5 x (4.5 - 1.7) = 12 + 7 = 19, raised. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 17 + 8 = 33.5, range 31.5 to 35.5; her 26 is below it, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis, which brings the pH back to normal in sepsis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 28, 49"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents in diabetic ketoacidosis after 2 days of vomiting. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 35 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 134 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 85 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). What is her anion gap?","choices":["6","20","30","49"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 134 - (85 + 19) = 134 - 104 = 30, far above 6 to 12. The change in gap (20) is much larger than the change in bicarbonate (25 - 19 = 6), so vomiting has added a metabolic alkalosis to the ketoacidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 30, 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had nasogastric suction for 4 days after bowel surgery. His arterial pH is 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and his bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). For metabolic alkalosis, carbon dioxide is expected to rise 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate, from baselines of 40 mm Hg and 25 mEq/L. What carbon dioxide is expected from appropriate compensation?","choices":["34 mm Hg","40 mm Hg","46 mm Hg","48 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate has risen 33 - 25 = 8 mEq/L. At 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L, carbon dioxide should rise 8 x 0.75 = 6, so the expected value is 40 + 6 = 46 mm Hg. In metabolic alkalosis the lungs compensate by retaining carbon dioxide. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 24, 53"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man is admitted after a crush injury with prolonged poor tissue perfusion. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which change should be expected from his acid-base disturbance itself?","choices":["Hydrogen ions move out of cells into the blood","Hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly, cutting delivery","Potassium moves out of cells, raising serum potassium","Potassium moves into cells, lowering serum potassium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.31 with a bicarbonate of 14 is a metabolic acidosis (poor perfusion and trauma cause lactic acidosis). In acidosis, hydrogen ions shift into cells and potassium shifts out of cells into the extracellular fluid, resulting in hyperkalemia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 45"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with lactic acidosis from postoperative sepsis receives an opioid by a pump and is now drowsy with shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.15 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 101 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which part of his history best explains his concurrent respiratory acidosis?","choices":["The postoperative sepsis","Anxiety after surgery","Saline given in surgery","The opioid pain medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28; his 36 is above it, a concurrent respiratory acidosis. Respiratory depressants such as narcotics cause decreased ventilation, matching his drowsiness and shallow breathing. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 32, 36"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with cirrhosis takes a diuretic and has a serum albumin 2.1 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.53 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 37 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 134 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 93 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). What is her anion gap corrected for albumin?","choices":["5","11","14.5","17"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 134 - (93 + 30) = 11. Albumin is 4.5 - 2.1 = 2.4 g/dL below 4.5, so add 2.5 x 2.4 = 6. Corrected gap = 11 + 6 = 17, above 6 to 12: a raised-gap acidosis is hidden under her metabolic alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 29"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has had profuse watery diarrhea for 6 days and is now hypotensive with cool extremities and a high lactate. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Raised-gap with normal-gap metabolic acidosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis alone","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (110 + 8) = 20, change in gap = 10; change in bicarbonate = 25 - 8 = 17. The bicarbonate fell more than the gap rose, and the high chloride of 110 accounts for the rest: lactic acidosis plus the normal-gap acidosis of diarrhea. Carbon dioxide 20 sits in the predicted 18 to 22 (1.5 x 8 + 8). — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 47, 50"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a flail chest (a segment of chest wall moving inward on inspiration) and is bleeding into his abdomen after a car crash. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.06 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 70 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 98 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in acute respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide. Is a second disorder present?","choices":["Yes, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Yes, a concurrent metabolic acidosis","No, appropriate acute compensation","Yes, a concurrent metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 1 x (70 - 40) / 10 = 28. His is 19, far below, and his gap = 139 - (98 + 19) = 22 is raised: a lactic acidosis from blood loss joins the respiratory acidosis of his flail chest, driving the pH to 7.06. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 24, 32, 33, 45"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with kidney failure has vomited for 3 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.39 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 39 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 92 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap, what is his change in anion gap?","choices":["2","13","23","33"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (92 + 23) = 23. Change in gap = 23 - 10 = 13. Against a change in bicarbonate of only 25 - 23 = 2, the gap rose far more, the sign of a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting on top of the uremic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 27, 30"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is sedated on a mechanical ventilator after abdominal surgery. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.55 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 26 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which ventilator change is most appropriate?","choices":["Leave the settings; mild alkalosis needs nothing","Lower the set rate or tidal volume","Raise the set rate or tidal volume","Give saline with potassium replacement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.55 with a carbon dioxide of 26 is a respiratory alkalosis, and on a ventilator the fix is to decrease minute volume by lowering the number of breaths per minute or the tidal volume, so less carbon dioxide is blown off. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 40"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has vomited repeatedly for 3 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which effect on oxygen delivery does her acid-base disorder produce?","choices":["Lower hemoglobin affinity; less oxygen delivered","Higher hemoglobin affinity; less oxygen delivered","Lower hemoglobin affinity; more oxygen delivered","Higher hemoglobin affinity; more oxygen delivered"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.48 with a bicarbonate of 34 is a metabolic alkalosis (vomiting loses gastric acid), and the carbon dioxide of 47 is respiratory compensation. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, so less oxygen is delivered to the tissues. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has renal tubular acidosis, irritable bowel syndrome with intermittent diarrhea, and glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor. His roommate found him confused beside an open container of antifreeze. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.23 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 22 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Ethylene glycol in the antifreeze","Renal tubular acidosis","Intermittent diarrhea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a bicarbonate of 9 is a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 140 - (9 + 105) = 26, well above 12. Ethylene glycol is a toxic alcohol on the raised-gap list. His other conditions all cause a normal-gap acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has weakness. Her blood pressure is normal, and she reports no vomiting and no medicines. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). The cause of her disorder is unclear. Which test best narrows the list of causes?","choices":["Serum anion gap","Serum albumin","Urine bicarbonate","Urine chloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.48 with a bicarbonate of 34 is a metabolic alkalosis. When its cause is unclear, measuring urine chloride separates the saline-responsive causes (low urine chloride) from the saline-unresponsive causes (high urine chloride). — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 51, 54"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with long-standing poor nutrition becomes confused with a low blood pressure and cool extremities. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 106 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her albumin is 2.1 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Which acid-base disorder do her results show?","choices":["Raised anion gap metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with renal compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and low bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis. The measured gap is 137 - (19 + 106) = 12, but her albumin is 2.4 g/dL below 4.5, adding 2.4 x 2.5 = 6, so the corrected gap is 18, above 12. Poor perfusion causes lactic acidosis, a raised-gap cause. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 11, 45, 57"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is evaluated for fatigue. Her arterial blood gas shows pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 40 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 24 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). A serum panel drawn at the same moment shows a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Use the panel bicarbonate and proceed","Average the two values and proceed","Recheck both, as the bicarbonates disagree","Use the gas bicarbonate and proceed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bicarbonate on the blood gas and on the electrolytes is compared to verify accuracy before any further step. A difference of 10 mEq/L between samples drawn together means one result is unreliable, so neither can carry the analysis yet. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and chronic carbon dioxide retention has an exacerbation. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 74 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His partial pressure of oxygen is 52 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100 mm Hg). Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Driving his carbon dioxide down rapidly","Treating the cause of the exacerbation","Giving oxygen in carefully titrated amounts","Reviewing his medicines for sedatives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His bicarbonate of 37 shows chronic renal compensation. Treatment is similar to acute disease but with careful oxygen, because a rapidly falling carbon dioxide in chronic respiratory acidosis may trigger the complications of alkalosis: cardiac arrhythmias, reduced cerebral perfusion and seizures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 36, 37"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, takes aspirin daily, takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, and has had profuse watery diarrhea for 5 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of her history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Aspirin","Isoniazid","Her diarrhea","Metformin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 138 - (15 + 114) = 9, normal, with a high chloride. Diarrhea, gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss, is one of the most common normal-gap causes. Metformin, aspirin and isoniazid all raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, hypertension treated with a thiazide, asthma treated with an inhaled bronchodilator, and kidney function that has worsened over a month. Serum ketones are negative. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of his medicines should be stopped because it adds to this acidosis?","choices":["No change to his medicines","Metformin","The thiazide","The bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a bicarbonate of 11 is a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 139 - (11 + 104) = 24, well above 12. Metformin is on the raised-gap list, and stopping possible contributors is part of treating the underlying cause. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a urinary infection is hypotensive, with cool, mottled skin, and is breathing fast. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.43 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 22 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 101 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which process best explains his raised anion gap?","choices":["Bicarbonate loss in diarrhea","Saline infusion","Lactic acidosis from poor perfusion","Hyperventilation from sepsis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 139 - (101 + 14) = 24, a raised-gap acidosis. Lactic acidosis from poor tissue perfusion is one of the big four raised-gap causes and fits his hypotension and mottling. His carbon dioxide of 22 is below the predicted 27 to 31 (1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2), a concurrent respiratory alkalosis of sepsis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 45, 26, 28"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease takes a diuretic and has a potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.42 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 61 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 38 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 88 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in chronic respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Give high-flow oxygen to lower carbon dioxide","Give sodium bicarbonate","Make no change since her pH is normal","Stop the diuretic and replace potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 3.5 x 2.1 = about 32; hers is 38, a metabolic alkalosis on top of chronic respiratory acidosis. Discontinuing contributors such as diuretics and treating potassium deficits are the listed steps for metabolic alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 34, 37, 54"}]};
